{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Let the earth sprout vegetation.</b> Here begins the account of the work of the third day. He clothed the earth with grasses and trees on the day he gathered the waters, before it dried out by means of the luminaries that he placed on the fourth day. Another interpretation: <b>Let the waters be gathered.</b> The work of the second day was already completed by means of the firmament that separated; and now begins the work of the third day, for if the waters are not gathered from upon the face of the earth, how could the dry land appear with sprouting grass and trees?" "<b>Fruit tree.</b> And it did not do so; and with the grasses it produced after their kinds more than it was commanded. From here one may learn that it intended for the good, for many trees would be lacking if the tree itself were like the fruit; and even so, when Adam was cursed for his sin, it too was remembered and cursed, for דבהדי כבשי דרחמנא למה לה — with the grinding of the miller, what is she (needed for)? The hand of HaShem is not shortened." "<b>Fruit.</b> Among the nouns written in singular form that have no singular — examples: שמש, טף, שכר, כר, בצק, בטן, דגן, תירוש ויצהר, and many more."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {2 "The Aramaic idiom דבהדי כבשי דרחמנא למה לה (literally: what does she have to do with the miller's grinding?) is a talmudic expression meaning: why should the earth share the blame? The idiom is left untranslated per the instruction that unaddressed quoted material stays unaddressed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:19:08.606002106Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}